## Architecture

```
app/
├── main.py              application assembly, middleware order
├── settings.py          typed config + production guardrails
├── db.py                async engine, session factory
├── auth/
│   ├── engine/          ← the ONLY place fastapi_users is imported
│   ├── routers/         HTTP surface
│   ├── security/        hashing, tokens, lockout, rate limits, audit
{%- if feature_orgs %}
│   ├── authz.py         roles, permissions, escalation guards
{%- endif %}
│   └── models.py        User, RefreshToken, AuditEvent{% if feature_sso %}, OAuthAccount{% endif %}{% if feature_orgs %}, Organization, Membership{% endif %}
└── email/               delivery + templates
```

### The engine seam

`fastapi-users` entered maintenance mode in March 2026 — still security-patched,
but no new features, with a successor toolkit in development. Every import of it
is confined to `app/auth/engine/`, enforced by a ruff rule and a CI check.
Migrating to a different engine later means rewriting that one package; routers,
models and tests do not move.

Import from `app.auth.engine`, never from `fastapi_users`:

```python
from {{ package_module }}.engine import current_active_user   # yes
from fastapi_users import ...                     # fails lint and CI
```

## Security

`docs/security.md` documents every control, why it exists, and what breaks
without it. Before going to production, work through the checklist at the end of
that file.

The short version of what is enforced by default:

- Argon2id password hashing (64 MiB, t=3, p=4) via `pwdlib`
- Refresh token rotation with **reuse detection** — a replayed token revokes the
  whole token family
- Purpose-bound JWT audiences, so a low-authority token cannot be replayed as an
  access token
- Uniform responses and timing on login and forgot-password, so neither
  enumerates accounts
- Progressive, capped account lockout plus Redis-backed rate limits
- {% if transport_cookie %}HttpOnly `__Host`-style cookies, double-submit CSRF, {% endif %}strict security headers
{%- if feature_orgs %}
- Per-organisation roles and permissions, with tenant isolation and privilege
  escalation guards — see `docs/security.md#authorization`
{%- endif %}
- Settings that refuse to boot with a placeholder secret, wildcard CORS, or
  debug mode in production

## Configuration

All configuration is environment-driven; `.env.example` lists every key.
`.env` was generated locally with real random secrets and is gitignored.

Rotate secrets with `basivo-auth secrets rotate` — note that this signs out
every user.

## Testing

```bash
uv run pytest                  # full suite
uv run pytest -m security      # only the security assertions
uv run pytest -m "not slow"    # skip container-backed tests
uv run pytest tests/test_tokens.py::test_reusing_a_rotated_token_revokes_the_entire_family
```
